<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zagros Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zagros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zagros Mountains are a long mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, stretching from the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and separating the Mesopotamian plain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. Their highland peoples repeatedly pressed on Mesopotamian states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. The Zagros was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, and its tribes, including the Zand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a>, formed the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains.jpg" alt="Zagros Mountains" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terpsichores · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoregion_PA0446.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
